  "id": "DrugBank_DB00201 - MONDO_6004",
  "verbalization": "Caffeine is implicated in the pathogenesis of Vasomotor Rhinitis through a series of indirect pathways, primarily via its association with Nasopharyngitis and Allergic Rhinitis. The evidence suggests that Caffeine may act as a modulator of inflammatory responses, potentially influencing the immune system and vascular tone, which are central to the development of Vasomotor Rhinitis. The shared indication of Nasopharyngitis in multiple paths indicates a possible role in the early stages of the condition, where inflammation and vascular dysfunction may be exacerbated by caffeine intake. Additionally, the link to Allergic Rhinitis, which is also associated with vasomotor symptoms, further supports the hypothesis that caffeine may influence airway inflammation and vascular reactivity. However, the exact mechanism by which caffeine exerts its effects remains unclear, and the evidence is limited to clinical indications rather than direct pharmacological action. The integration of these pathways suggests that caffeine may contribute to the pathophysiology of Vasomotor Rhinitis through inflammatory and vascular mechanisms, though further mechanistic studies are needed to confirm this relationship.",
